    "summary": "High‑energy set with Touch of Grey debut and soaring Morning Dew",
    "blurb": "The September 17 1982 Portland show bursts with a blistering first set that launches into an inspired second half. Highlights include the debut of Touch of Grey, a fiery Spanish Jam → The Other One sequence, and a transcendent Morning Dew. Both the band’s tight interplay and the high‑quality soundboard capture make it a must‑listen.",
    "review": "What unfolds at the Cumberland County Civic Center is a quintessential early‑'80 Dead night that captures the band in full stride. Set one rides a smooth groove from a classic Bertha opener through crisp renditions of Candyman > C.C. Rider and a tight Throwing Stones → Deal transition, laying down a solid foundation for what becomes an explosive second set. The band dives into Touch of Grey—its first live appearance—then rolls into a manic Man Smart, Woman Smarter before soaring through High Time and the Estimated Prophet > Eyes of the World stretch. From there the rhythm section erupts: drums and Space give way to a tense Spanish Jam that morphs seamlessly into The Other One, propelling into Goin' Down The Road Feeling Bad and an unforgettable Morning Dew that rides on Jerry’s impassioned vocals and Phil’s “bass melt‑down.” The set closes with Sugar Magnolia and a rousing U.S. Blues encore. Across the board the playing is tight yet adventurous; Brent’s keyboards, though occasionally muted by a missing mic, add a bright country‑western flavor, while Mickey and Bill lock in a thunderous dual‑drum assault that pushes the jams forward. The soundboard source (Harrison) captures this energy with clarity and depth, making it the definitive listening choice.",
